# GoCluster Release Package

This ZIP contains the Windows amd64 executable, public example configuration,
selected runtime data, and an operator documentation bundle.

The cluster executable writes peer diagnostic files. `binaries.json` records
the executable hash; `licenses/` includes the
topology SQLite fork and engine notices, and the Go BSD notice for adapted
bounded path resolution.

## Configure

The packaged `data/config` directory is public example config. For a real node:

1. Copy `data/config` to a private complete config directory such as
   `data/config.local`.
2. Edit the private copy with your node ID, telnet port, spot sources, peer
   hosts/passwords, and service tokens.
3. Keep private config out of Git and out of shared zip archives.

Minimum files to review:

- `app.yaml`: server identity, UI/headless mode, and logging paths.
- `runtime.yaml`: telnet port, default filters, buffers, and Go runtime controls.
- `ingest.yaml`: RBN, PSKReporter, DXSummit, and human/manual ingest settings.
- `peering.yaml`: only if peering with other clusters is used.
- `reputation.yaml`: only if IPinfo/Cymru enrichment is enabled.
- `solarweather.yaml`: only if solar/geomagnetic path overrides are enabled.
- `data.yaml`: data paths when this deployment layout differs.
- `spot_taxonomy.yaml`: only when changing modes, events, or PSKReporter mode routing.

The config loader expects a complete directory. If you use `DXC_CONFIG_PATH`,
point it at the whole private config directory, not at one YAML file.

At minimum, replace the public placeholder identity before connecting a real
node: change `server.node_id` in `app.yaml` from `N0CALL-1`, change the RBN
login callsigns in `ingest.yaml` from `N0CALL-1`, and update any private
upstream telnet `host` and login fields you enable. If peering is enabled,
also replace peer hosts, login callsigns, and passwords in `peering.yaml`.

## Check Runtime Data

Before a fresh start, confirm that the active `pipeline.yaml` model files
exist. The supplied configuration refers to three spotter-reliability files
and a confusion model under `data/rbn/priors`. If the ZIP omits them, copy them
from the matching tagged source. See the [download guide](https://github.com/N2WQ/GoCluster/blob/main/download/README.md)
for the paths required by release 261121r74c2.

Use the release tag in `data/config-bundle.json` to open matching source docs
for references outside the ZIP.

## Launch

From PowerShell in the extracted `ready_to_run` directory:

```pwsh
$env:DXC_CONFIG_PATH = "data/config.local"
.\gocluster.exe
```

If you are only testing the public example config, omit `DXC_CONFIG_PATH` and
run `.\gocluster.exe` directly.

## Connect

Use a telnet client against the configured telnet port, then log in with your
callsign:

```text
telnet localhost {{ .TelnetPort }}
```

After login, type `HELP` for common tasks or `HELP COMMANDS` for the full index.

- `SHOW MYDX` or `SHOW DX`: show filtered spot history.
- `WHOSPOTSME [band]`: show recent spotter countries for your call.
- `SET GRID <grid>`: set your 4 or 6 character Maidenhead grid.
- `SHOW FILTER`: display active filters.
- `HELP FILTERS` explains how to change filters in your active dialect.
- `SHOW DEDUPE` and `SET DEDUPE FAST|MED|SLOW`: inspect or change duplicate suppression.
- `SET DIAG OFF|DEDUPE|SOURCE|CONF|PATH|MODE`: replace spot comments with compact per-session diagnostics.
- `SET SOLAR 15|30|60|OFF`: opt into or stop periodic solar summaries.
- `DIALECT`, `DIALECT LIST`, `DIALECT <go|cc>`: show or switch command dialect.
- `BYE`: disconnect.

### Meta Bands

Meta bands select fixed groups of amateur bands. For example, `WARC` selects
30m, 17m, and 12m. Other groups are `HFCONTEST`, `HF`, `VHF`, `UHF`, and
`VHFUP`. Type `HELP BANDS` for the full list and filter examples in GO and CC.
To find up to 20 recent WARC spots that match your filters, type
`SHOW DX 20 BAND WARC` in GO or `SHOW/DX 20 BAND WARC` in CC.

### Your Operating Geography

In GO, `DECONT=MYCONT` selects reports from spotters on your operating
continent. `DXDXCC=MYDXCC` selects stations in your operating DXCC entity.
For example, CTY assigns North America and Canada (ADIF `1`) to `N2WQ/VE3`
for that connection. Saved rules keep the variable names, so the expression
can follow the operator who uses it. Type `HELP RULE` for field and list syntax.

## Linux

This release package is Windows amd64 only. Linux operators should build from
source with Go `1.27.1+`:

```sh
GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build -trimpath -o gocluster .
```

Deploy the Linux executable with the required
runtime data from the source tree, including `data/cty`, `data/h3`, `data/peers/topology.db`, and
`data/skm_correction/rbnskew.json` when those inputs are used by your config.

For `systemd`, create a dedicated service account, install the binary, config,
and runtime data under a stable path such as `/opt/gocluster`, assign ownership
to that account, save the unit as `/etc/systemd/system/gocluster.service`, set
`DXC_CONFIG_PATH`, and use `ui.mode: headless` in `app.yaml`. Service logs are
available with:

```sh
journalctl -u gocluster -f
```

The interactive local console requires `ui.mode: tview-v2` and a real terminal,
not a normal unattended service.

Propagation/path aggregate logs are written to `logging.propagation.dir`
(`data/logs/propagation` in the shipped config), not to the system log. The
active file is `propagation.log`; completed UTC days archive as
`DD-Mon-YYYY.log`. The `prop_report` tool reads the completed propagation
archive by default; use `-log` for historical system log files.

